<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pavel Bermondt-Avalov was a Russian officer and adventurer who in 1919 commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-russian-volunteer-army">West Russian Volunteer Army</a>, a force of anti-Bolshevik Russians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">German Freikorps</a> soldiers in Latvia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> backed by German commanders. In October 1919 he attacked Riga but was repulsed by Latvian forces with Estonian and Allied naval help in November, and his army was also defeated by Lithuanian troops near Radviliškis. He spent the rest of his life in emigration.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pavel-bermondt-avalov.jpg" alt="Pavel Bermondt-Avalov" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavel_Bermondt-Avalov,_c._1920,_US_Library_of_Congress_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
